258 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 116 0 R
/E (This study helped in understanding the evolution of a system from a disordered state to an ordered state following a quench. The formation and growth of domains were studied using the Ising model and phase separation in binary liquids. The role of the order parameter and the characteristic length scale L\(t\) was examined through the scaling hypothesis, which provides a way to describe the domain structure at late times.  )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
259 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 116 0 R
/E (The study also provided an understanding of domain walls and how their curvature and surface tension drive domain growth. For non conserved dynamics, domain walls move due to curvature, leading to the reduction of domain-wall area. For conserved dynamics, the order parameter cannot change locally and domain growth occurs through diffusion driven by differences in chemical potential.  )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
260 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 116 0 R
/E (Overall, the study showed how the mathematical descriptions of conserved and non-conserved dynamics lead to different mechanisms of domain growth. The concepts of scaling, domain-wall motion, diffusion, and chemical potential provide a framework for understanding the long-time evolution of phase separating systems. )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
